  "full_prompt": "Create an animation explaining backpropagation, the algorithm that computes the gradient of the cost function. Start with a brief recap of the network architecture (784 input neurons, hidden layers with 16 neurons each, 10 output neurons) and gradient descent. Introduce backpropagation as the algorithm for computing the gradient efficiently. Walk through the intuitive effects of a single training example on weights and biases. Start with an example image of a '2' and show the desired output layer: the neuron for 2 should be nudged up, all others nudged down, with nudge sizes proportional to how far each is from its target. Show that we can't directly change activations - only weights and biases. Then focus on a single output neuron (the one for 2) and show the three ways to increase its activation: increase bias, increase weights (with those connected to brighter previous-layer neurons having more influence), and change previous layer activations (in proportion to weights). Show the 'fire together, wire together' analogy. Demonstrate that all output neurons' desires are added together to determine changes to the previous layer. Show the recursive propagation backward through the network. Explain that this gives desired changes for one training example, and we average these across all training examples to get the gradient. Introduce mini-batches and stochastic gradient descent as a computational efficiency technique. End with a summary of the backpropagation algorithm in plain language.",
